SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEK AHEAD
Sunday, March 22, 2026

What we are watching this week.

Monday: Durable goods orders for February. Core capital goods orders have risen four months straight; a fifth would be the longest streak since 2021 and would confirm what the LTL carriers are saying about industrial demand.

Tuesday: The FMC's open meeting, where the Commission is due to vote on whether to open a formal inquiry into the container-lessor merger. Also the Port of Oakland's March volume preview.

Wednesday: The final GDP revision for Q4. Nobody in freight cares about GDP, but the inventory-investment component is the best quarterly read of whether businesses are building stock.

Thursday: The February trade balance, with the country-level import detail that shows where sourcing has actually moved, as opposed to where the press releases say it has.

Friday: Personal income and spending for February, and the last trading day of the quarter, which is when a surprising number of annual freight contracts quietly renew.

Also: the largest US 3PL's investor day on Tuesday; a hearing on the proposed federal truck-parking grant program; and, for the Bay Area readers, the start of stone-fruit season in earnest, with the first full reefer trains out of Fresno.

See you tomorrow at 6.

